# Non-negotiable production engineering rules

1. Never patch, hack, bypass, or workaround a defect.

2. Always fix the actual root cause with a proper production implementation.

3. Never introduce temporary fixes, temporary compatibility code, or “fix for now” logic.

4. Never modify anything outside the explicitly authorised scope.

5. Never change UI, layout, styling, positioning, icons, colours, navigation, or user-visible behaviour unless explicitly authorised.

6. Never modify working functionality unless it is proven necessary for the authorised task.

7. Never replace working architecture with a different, simplified, temporary, or duplicate architecture.

8. Never create duplicate implementations of existing functionality.

9. Every subsystem must have one clear production owner and one source of truth.

10. Never create duplicate API clients, WebSockets, LiveKit owners, authentication owners, stores, services, hooks, wallet logic, IAP logic, gift logic, navigation logic, timers, listeners, or state owners.

11. Never silently swallow production errors.

12. Never use empty `catch` blocks or `.catch(() => {})` unless the error is proven harmless and intentionally ignored.

13. Never return fake success when an operation actually failed.

14. Never invent fallback data, balances, responses, API results, events, routes, database fields, environment variables, or server behaviour.

15. Never hide server, database, payment, authentication, or network failures by returning empty/default data unless that behaviour is intentionally required.

16. Never delete code simply because it looks unused.

17. Never delete a file, function, service, route, script, dependency, export, or feature without proving it is genuinely unused.

18. Disconnected functionality is not automatically dead functionality.

19. Never remove intended functionality instead of properly reconnecting or repairing it.

20. Never replace one patch or workaround with another patch or workaround.

21. Existing patches and workarounds must not be considered permanent architecture.

22. When a patch or workaround is encountered, identify the underlying reason it exists and preserve required functionality while replacing it with the correct production implementation.

23. The final production code must not contain unnecessary patches, workarounds, hacks, temporary fixes, compatibility shims, emergency fallbacks, or bypasses.

24. The final production code must not contain unnecessary dead code, abandoned code, stale experiments, commented-out implementations, duplicate implementations, or obsolete files.

25. Never leave TODO, FIXME, HACK, TEMP, temporary, workaround, fallback, or placeholder production logic unresolved when it represents unfinished production work.

26. Never leave production stubs pretending to be completed functionality.

27. Never present placeholder functionality as production-ready functionality.

28. Never leave test-only behaviour accessible in production.

29. Never expose development-only routes, debug controls, test credentials, test transactions, test coins, mocks, or fake data in production.

30. Never mix test or promotional value with real financial value.

31. Never mix separate payment systems or transaction paths unless the architecture explicitly requires it.

32. Never locally invent or adjust financial balances to compensate for failed server operations.

33. Never bypass proper server verification for purchases, subscriptions, gifts, payouts, wallets, or financial transactions.

34. Never weaken authentication, authorization, rate limiting, fraud prevention, validation, or production security merely to prevent an error.

35. Never use fail-open security behaviour unless it is explicitly intentional, documented, and required by the production architecture.

36. Never add a new dependency when the existing production architecture already provides the required capability.

37. Never introduce an external SaaS, CDN, API, database, storage service, payment provider, analytics provider, or infrastructure dependency without explicit authorisation.

38. Never fabricate or change frontend/backend contracts to make one side compile.

39. Client and server contracts must remain consistent with the real production implementation.

40. Never create raw string event names when an existing canonical event/constants contract exists.

41. Never create a second network path to bypass the application's existing transport owner.

42. Never create a second state source merely to solve a local problem.

43. Never use local storage as an unnecessary second source of truth when authoritative server/application state already exists.

44. Never introduce unnecessary polling, duplicate listeners, duplicate loops, duplicate subscriptions, or duplicate lifecycle ownership.

45. Never leave race conditions knowingly unresolved.

46. Never leave resource leaks, stale listeners, abandoned timers, unclosed sockets, uncleaned media tracks, or unhandled subscriptions.

47. Never make broad refactors while performing a narrowly authorised fix.

48. Never rename, move, reorganise, or reformat unrelated code merely because it looks cleaner.

49. Never perform opportunistic changes outside the authorised requirement.

50. Never make assumptions about code ownership or runtime behaviour without inspecting the existing implementation.

51. Before modifying code, understand its callers, dependencies, contracts, state ownership, runtime lifecycle, and side effects.

52. Never claim something is fixed merely because TypeScript compiles.

53. Never claim something is clean merely because lint passes.

54. Never claim something is unused merely because a static-analysis tool reports it.

55. Scanner findings are evidence to investigate, not automatic permission to delete or rewrite code.

56. Never suppress compiler, lint, test, security, or scanner findings simply to make a gate green.

57. Never weaken tests to make broken code pass.

58. Never remove tests because implementation changes caused them to fail unless the tested behaviour itself has been explicitly removed.

59. Never disable validation, checks, rules, or safeguards merely to complete a task.

60. Never commit code with known unresolved failures in required validation gates.

61. Never work directly on the protected release branch or `main` when an isolated working branch can be used.

62. Never merge AI-generated changes automatically into the release branch.

63. Every change must remain reviewable through Git diff and Git history.

64. Never rewrite or destroy Git history to hide mistakes or previous changes.

65. Never delete evidence, logs, reports, tests, or history simply because they expose previous defects.

66. Never modify protected files or protected areas without explicit authorisation.

67. If a correct implementation requires changing a protected area, stop editing that area and report the dependency instead of bypassing the rule.

68. Never interpret “make it work” as permission to ignore architecture, security, production quality, or scope restrictions.

69. Never interpret “clean the code” as permission to redesign the application.

70. Never interpret “remove leftovers” as permission to remove real functionality.

71. Never interpret “release-ready” as permission to hide unresolved problems.

72. Never mark a known stub, workaround, placeholder, architecture risk, or unresolved production dependency as complete.

73. Never state that the codebase is patch-free, workaround-free, clean, or release-ready unless that statement is supported by the actual current codebase.

74. Never hide remaining risks or unresolved findings from the final status.

75. Never falsely report a test, build, scanner, native build, server check, deployment check, or runtime check as passed if it was not actually run successfully.

76. Never infer runtime success solely from static code inspection.

77. Never change production behaviour merely to satisfy a test when the test exposes a genuine defect.

78. Never sacrifice maintainability, correctness, security, data integrity, or architecture for speed.

79. Never prioritise completing the task over preserving working functionality.

80. The required standard is clean production engineering — not patches, not workarounds, not temporary code, not hidden failures, and not unfinished implementations.
